# Hackedbox Startup Directory

Hackedbox provides a simple startup directory for programs and scripts
that should be started when the Hackedbox session begins.

The startup system is intentionally simple. There is no special
configuration language, no numbered startup files, and no dependency
system. Startup entries are ordinary shell scripts using the `.run`
extension.

## How It Works:

When Hackedbox starts, `hackedbox-session` checks:

~/.hackedbox/startup/

It runs each executable regular file matching:

*.run

Each startup file is run in the background before Hackedbox starts.

## Startup Scripts:

Startup files are ordinary shell scripts.

For example:

#!/bin/sh

volumeicon &

Save the script as:

~/.hackedbox/startup/sound.run

Then make it executable:

chmod +x ~/.hackedbox/startup/sound.run

The `.run` extension is required for `hackedbox-session` to recognize
the file as a startup entry.

## Starting User Programs:

The startup directory can also be used to start graphical programs
automatically when the Hackedbox session begins.

For example:

#!/bin/sh

conky &
xclock &
my-program &

Save the script as:

~/.hackedbox/startup/programs.run

Then make it executable:

chmod +x ~/.hackedbox/startup/programs.run

Anything that can normally be started from a terminal can be placed in
a startup script.

## Starting Session Services:

The startup directory can also be used to start user-session services
that are needed by the graphical environment.

For example:

#!/bin/sh

/sbin/openrc -U default

Save it as:

~/.hackedbox/startup/openrc.run

Then make it executable:

chmod +x ~/.hackedbox/startup/openrc.run

This allows user-session services to be started as part of the
Hackedbox graphical session without making Hackedbox responsible for
managing those services.

## Startup Programs and System Services:

The Hackedbox startup directory is not a replacement for the system
init system.

OpenRC, runit, dinit, s6, systemd, or another init system should
continue to manage system services.

The Hackedbox startup directory is intended for graphical-session
programs, user-session services, and other user-specific startup tasks.

Typical uses include:

volume and tray applications
network tray applications
desktop utilities
notification programs
user applications
graphical configuration programs
user-session services
scripts needed by the graphical session

Hackedbox does not manage the services started by these scripts. It
simply executes the startup entries when the graphical session begins.

## Keep It Simple:

The startup system follows a simple rule:

Executable *.run files in ~/.hackedbox/startup/ are run when the
Hackedbox session starts.

There are no startup numbers, no complicated dependency system, and no
Hackedbox-specific scripting language.

Just normal shell scripts in one directory.
